      ],
      "margin_number": 145,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(a) The meaning of the second aorist is the same as the first aorist: ‘I -ed’ (or sometimes ‘I have -ed’ or ‘I had -ed’: see 142).\n(b) Note the -μην -σο -το middle endings (102).\n(c) The endings of the second aorist are exactly the same as those of the imperfect. The difference between the two tenses lies in the change of stem:",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": 145,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "box",
      "order": 5,
      "bbox": [
        83,
        403,
        907,
        451
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Second aorists have a stem or a form of the stem which is different from that of the present whereas the imperfect is based on the present stem.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": "►",
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
